      <h2>What happens now.</h2>
      <p><strong>Business day 1:</strong> Office calls you to scope the project. If you sent a specific equipment request or photos, we review them before the call. Most scoping calls run 15–25 minutes.</p>
      <p><strong>Business day 2–5:</strong> On-site visit (60–90 minutes). We measure envelope, pressurize the duct system if applicable, note existing conditions, and take photos for the load calc.</p>
      <p><strong>Business day 5–8:</strong> Manual J load calculation completed. Equipment modeled against your load. Itemized proposal drafted.</p>
      <p><strong>Business day 8–10:</strong> Proposal delivered by email with 2–3 equipment options, each priced, each showing rebates/credits applied. We schedule a 20-minute review call to walk through the options.</p>

      <h3>If this is an emergency.</h3>
      <p>If your system is down during cold-snap or heat-wave weather, hang up from this site and call <a href="tel:9283159094">(928) 315-9094</a> directly and press 9. We prioritize no-heat situations below 20°F and no-cool above 95°F.</p>

      <h3>Before the site visit.</h3>
      <p>You don't need to prep anything. But if you want to help the scope happen faster:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Note your current furnace and AC model numbers (stickers on the equipment).</li>
        <li>Find your last full year of utility bills, it helps calibrate the load calc.</li>
        <li>List any rooms that never feel right (too cold, too hot, slow to change).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>None of that is required. We'll figure it out either way.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

